window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_alternatingcurrent=[
{"id":"neet-2026-q007","pn":7,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Series LCR resonance","q":"An ac circuit contains a resistance of 1 kΩ, a capacitor of 0.1 μF and an inductor of 1 mH connected in series. The resonance frequency of the circuit is approximately.","o":["10.1 kHz","20.7 kHz","15.9 kHz","13.5 kHz"],"c":2,"s":"Resonance frequency f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC)).\nf₀ = 1/(2π√(1×10⁻⁷ × 10⁻³)).\nf₀ = 15.9 kHz."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q012","pn":12,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Series LCR resonance","q":"An ac voltage V = 220 sin(2 × 10³t) Volt is applied to a series LCR circuit. Then the current amplitude in this circuit is:\n(Given : L = 10 mH, C = 25 μF, R = 100 Ω)","o":["5.5 A","11.0 A","22.0 A","2.2 A"],"c":3,"s":"Angular frequency of the source ω = 2 × 10³ rad/s.\nInductive reactance X_L = ωL = (2 × 10³) × (10 × 10⁻³) = 20 Ω.\nCapacitive reactance X_C = 1/(ωC) = 1/[(2 × 10³) × (25 × 10⁻⁶)] = 20 Ω.\nX_L = X_C, so the circuit is at resonance and impedance Z = R.\nCurrent amplitude i₀ = V₀/Z = V₀/R = 220/100 = 2.2 A."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q033","pn":33,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"AC instantaneous value","q":"The peak value of an alternating current is 5 A and frequency is 60 Hz. How long will the current, starting from zero, take to reach the peak value?","o":["1/240 s","1/30 s","1/120 s","1/60 s"],"c":0,"s":"Alternating current: i = i_peak sin(ωt).\nHere ω = 2πf = 2π × 60 = 120π rad/s.\nSo i = 5 sin(120πt).\nFor the peak value 5 = 5 sin(120πt), so sin(120πt) = 1 = sin(π/2).\nHence 120πt = π/2 and t = 1/240 s."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q016","pn":16,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Series LCR circuit","q":"To an ac power supply of 220 V at 50 Hz, a resistor of 20 Ω, a capacitor of reactance 25 Ω and an inductor of reactance 45 Ω are connected in series. The corresponding current in the circuit and the phase angle between the current and the voltage is, respectively","o":["7.8 A and 30°","7.8 A and 45°","15.6 A and 30°","15.6 A and 45°"],"c":1,"s":"X_L = 45 Ω, X_C = 25 Ω, R = 20 Ω\nI = 220/√((X_L − X_C)² + R²) = 220/√((45 − 25)² + 20²)\nI = 220/(2√2) = 11/√2 = 7.779 A\ntan φ = (X_L − X_C)/R = (45 − 25)/20 = 1\nφ = 45°"},
{"id":"neet-2024-q005","pn":5,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Transformer turns ratio","q":"In an ideal transformer, the turns ratio is Nₚ/Nₛ = 1/2. The ratio Vₛ : Vₚ is equal to (the symbols carry their usual meaning) -","o":["1 : 1","1 : 4","1 : 2","2 : 1"],"c":3,"s":"For a transformer, Vₛ/Vₚ = Nₛ/Nₚ.\nNₚ/Nₛ = 1/2, so Nₛ/Nₚ = 2.\nSo Vₛ : Vₚ = 2 : 1."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q045","pn":45,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Capacitor in AC circuit","q":"A 10 μF capacitor is connected to a 210 V, 50 Hz source as shown in figure. The peak current in the circuit is nearly (π = 3.14):","o":["1.20 A","0.35 A","0.58 A","0.93 A"],"c":3,"s":"Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(ωC) = 1/(2πfC) = 1/(2 × 3.14 × 50 × 10 × 10⁻⁶) = 1000/3.14 Ω.\nV_rms = 210 V, so i_rms = V_rms/X_C = 210/X_C.\nPeak current = √2 × i_rms = √2 × (210/1000) × 3.14 = 0.932 ≈ 0.93 A.","fig":{"w":240,"h":205,"alt":"A capacitor C = 10 μF in the top wire of a rectangular circuit, left plate marked plus and right plate minus, and an AC source 210 V, 50 Hz in the bottom wire.","d":[["l",15,28,128,28],["l",143,28,235,28],["l",128,12,128,46],["l",143,12,143,46],["t",124,24,"+","e","s"],["t",124,41,"+","e","s"],["t",148,24,"−","s"],["t",148,41,"−","s"],["t",144,66,"C = 10 μF","m"],["l",15,28,15,167],["l",235,28,235,167],["l",15,167,87,167],["c",95,167,8],["t",95,170,"~","ms"],["l",103,167,235,167],["t",88,193,"210 V, 50 Hz"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2023-q026","pn":26,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Resonance in LCR circuit","q":"In a series LCR circuit, the inductance L is 10 mH, capacitance C is 1 µF and resistance R is 100 Ω. The frequency at which resonance occurs is:","o":["1.59 kHz","15.9 rad/s","15.9 kHz","1.59 rad/s"],"c":0,"s":"At resonance the frequency is f = 1/(2π√(LC)).\nLC = 10×10⁻³ × 1×10⁻⁶ = 10⁻⁸, so √(LC) = 10⁻⁴.\nf = 10⁴/(2π) = 1.591×10³ Hz = 1.591 kHz."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q027","pn":27,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Capacitor in ac circuit","q":"An ac source is connected to a capacitor C. Due to decrease in its operating frequency:","o":["capacitive reactance remains constant","capacitive reactance decreases.","displacement current increases.","displacement current decreases."],"c":3,"s":"Capacitive reactance is X_C = 1/(ωC).\nWhen the frequency falls, ω falls, so X_C increases.\nThe current in the circuit therefore decreases.\nConduction current equals displacement current, so the displacement current decreases."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q029","pn":29,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Transformer","q":"A 12 V, 60 W lamp is connected to the secondary of a step-down transformer, whose primary is connected to ac mains of 220 V. Assuming the transformer to be ideal, what is the current in the primary winding?","o":["3.7 A","0.27 A","2.7 A","0.37 A"],"c":1,"s":"For an ideal transformer, input power = output power.\nV×I in the primary = 60 W.\n220 × I = 60, so I ≈ 0.27 A."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q044","pn":44,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Impedance of LCR circuit","q":"The net impedance of circuit (as shown in figure) will be :","o":["25 Ω","10√2 Ω","15 Ω","5√5 Ω"],"c":3,"s":"L = (50/π) mH, so X_L = 2π×50×(50/π)×10⁻³ = 5 Ω.\nC = (10³/π) µF, so X_C = 1/(2π×50×(10³/π)×10⁻⁶) = 10 Ω.\nZ = √((X_C − X_L)² + R²) = √((10 − 5)² + 10²) = √125 = 5√5 Ω.","fig":{"w":250,"h":135,"alt":"Series circuit: an inductor of 50/π mH, a capacitor of 10³/π µF and a 10 Ω resistor along the top, and an ac source (220 V, 50 Hz) at the bottom.","d":[["l",11,20,40,20],["p","M40 20A4 5 0 0 1 48 20A4 5 0 0 1 56 20A4 5 0 0 1 64 20A4 5 0 0 1 72 20"],["l",72,20,105,20],["l",105,11,105,29],["l",111,11,111,29],["l",111,20,160,20],["p","M160 20H163L165.42 16L170.25 24L175.08 16L179.92 24L184.75 16L189.58 24L192 20H195"],["l",195,20,225,20],["l",11,20,11,105],["l",225,20,225,105],["l",11,105,100,105],["l",124,105,225,105],["c",112,105,12,""],["p","M104 105C108 99 110 99 112 105S116 111 120 105"],["t",46,42,"50/π mH","m","s"],["t",122,42,"10³/π µF","m","s"],["t",185,42,"10Ω","m"],["t",135,126,"(220V, 50Hz)"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q014","pn":14,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"AC through a capacitor","q":"Given below are two statements.\nStatement I: In an ac circuit, the current through a capacitor leads the voltage across it.\nStatement II: In a.c. circuits containing pure capacitance only, the phase difference between the current and the voltage is π.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer form the options given below","o":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are Incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"c":3,"s":"In an AC circuit the current through a capacitor leads the voltage across it by a phase π/2.\nSo Statement I is correct.\nStatement II says π, which is wrong."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q017","pn":17,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"RMS and peak value","q":"The peak voltage of the ac source is equal to","o":["The rms value of the ac source","√2 times the rms value of the ac source","1/√2 times the rms value of the ac source","The value of voltage supplied to the circuit"],"c":1,"s":"The rms value is E_rms = E₀/√2.\nSo the peak value is E₀ = √2 E_rms."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q021","pn":21,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Inductive reactance","q":"An inductor of inductance 2 mH is connected to a 220 V, 50 Hz a.c. source. Let the inductive reactance in the circuit is X₁. If a 220 V dc source replaces the ac source in the circuit, then the inductive reactance in the circuit is X₂. X₁ and X₂ respectively are","o":["0.628 Ω, infinity","6.25 Ω, zero","6.28 Ω, infinity","0.628 Ω, zero"],"c":3,"s":"With the AC source: X₁ = ωL = 2πfL = 2π × 50 × 2 × 10⁻³ = 0.628 Ω.\nWith a DC source f = 0, so X₂ = 2πfL = 0."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q034","pn":34,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Peak and rms current","q":"A standard filament lamp consumes 100 W when connected to 200 V ac mains supply. The peak current through the bulb will be:","o":["2A","0.707 A","1A","1.414"],"c":1,"s":"P = 100 W and V_rms = 200 V.\nI_rms = P/V_rms = 100/200 = 1/2 A.\nI₀ = I_rms × √2 = 0.707 A."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q045","pn":45,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Resonance in a series LCR circuit","q":"A series LCR circuit with inductance 10 H, capacitance 10 μF, resistance 50 Ω is connected to an ac source of voltage, V = 200 sin(100t) volt. If the resonant frequency of the LCR circuit is ν₀ and the frequency of the ac source is ν, then","o":["ν₀ = ν = 50/π Hz","ν₀ = 50/π Hz, ν = 50 Hz","ν = 100 Hz, ν₀ = 100/π Hz","ν₀ = ν = 50 Hz"],"c":0,"s":"At resonance ωL = 1/(ωC).\nω₀ = 1/√(LC) = 1/√(10 × 10 × 10⁻⁶) = 100 rad/s.\nUsing ω = 2πf, ν₀ = 100/(2π) = 50/π Hz.\nThe source V = 200 sin(100t) also has ω = 100, so ν = 100/(2π) = 50/π Hz.\nSo ν₀ = ν = 50/π Hz."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q016","pn":16,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"LCR series circuit","q":"An inductor of inductance L, a capacitor of capacitance C and a resistor of resistance 'R' are connected in series to an ac source of potential difference 'V' volts as shown in figure. Potential difference across L, C and R is 40 V, 10 V and 40 V, respectively. The amplitude of current flowing through LCR series circuit is 10√2 A. The impedance of the circuit is","o":["5 Ω","4√2 Ω","5/√2 Ω","4 Ω"],"c":0,"s":"VL and VC are opposite in phase, so net reactive voltage = 40 – 10 = 30 V.\nSource voltage V = √(VR² + (VL – VC)²) = √(40² + 30²) = 50 V (rms).\nIrms = I₀/√2 = 10√2/√2 = 10 A.\nZ = V/I = 50/10 = 5 Ω.","fig":{"w":330,"h":170,"alt":"Series loop with an inductor, a capacitor and a resistor on the top wire and an ac source V at the bottom; voltages 40 V, 10 V and 40 V marked across them","d":[["p","M30 30 H65 C65 18 77 18 77 30 C77 18 89 18 89 30 C89 18 101 18 101 30 C101 18 113 18 113 30 H150"],["l",150,19,150,41],["l",157,19,157,41],["l",157,30,230,30],["p","M230 30 H234 L238 20 L244 40 L250 20 L256 40 L262 20 L266 30 H300"],["l",300,30,300,125],["l",30,30,30,125],["l",30,125,150,125],["l",172,125,300,125],["c",161,125,11],["p","M155 125 C158 119 160 119 161 125 C162 131 164 131 167 125"],["t",161,152,"V","m"],["l",78,86,31,86,"a"],["l",102,86,149,86,"a"],["t",90,90,"40 V","ms"],["l",172,86,158,86,"a"],["l",204,86,214,86,"a"],["t",188,90,"10 V","ms"],["l",258,86,216,86,"a"],["l",286,86,299,86,"a"],["t",272,90,"40 V","ms"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2021-q045","pn":45,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Transformer","q":"A step down transformer connected to an ac mains supply of 220 V is made to operate at 11 V, 44 W lamp. Ignoring power losses in the transformer, what is the current in the primary circuit?","o":["4 A","0.2 A","0.4 A","2 A"],"c":1,"s":"No losses: input power = output power = 44 W.\nVp Ip = 44, so Ip = 44/220 = 0.2 A."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q047","pn":47,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Resonance and half-power frequencies","q":"A series LCR circuit containing 5.0 H inductor, 80 μF capacitor and 40 Ω resistor is connected to 230 V variable frequency ac source. The angular frequencies of the source at which power transferred to the circuit is half the power at the resonant angular frequency are likely to be-","o":["42 rad/s and 58 rad/s","25 rad/s and 75 rad/s","50 rad/s and 25 rad/s","46 rad/s and 54 rad/s"],"c":3,"s":"Resonant frequency: ω₀ = 1/√(LC) = 1/√(5 × 80 × 10⁻⁶) = 1/√(4 × 10⁻⁴) = 50 rad/s.\nPower is half at the two frequencies where the current is 1/√2 of its peak; for a sharp resonance these are ω₀ ± R/(2L).\nR/(2L) = 40/(2 × 5) = 4 rad/s.\nSo ω = 50 – 4 = 46 rad/s and 50 + 4 = 54 rad/s."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q108","pn":108,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"LCR circuit power factor","q":"A series LCR circuit is connected to an voltage source. When L is removed from the circuit, the phase difference between current and voltage is π/3. If instead C is removed from the circuit, the phase difference is again π/3 between current and voltage. The power factor of the circuit is :","o":["0.5","1.0","–1.0","zero"],"c":1,"s":"Without L: tan φ = X_C/R. Without C: tan φ = X_L/R.\nThe phase differences are equal (π/3), so X_C = X_L.\nSo the circuit is in resonance and Z = R.\nPower factor = cos φ = R/Z = 1."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q123","pn":123,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Alternating Current","st":"Capacitor in AC circuit","q":"A 40 μF capacitor is connected to a 200 V, 50 Hz ac supply. The rms value of the current in the circuit is, nearly :","o":["2.05 A","2.5 A","25.1 A","1.7 A"],"c":1,"s":"Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(ωC), so I = V/X_C = VωC.\nω = 2π × 50 = 314 rad/s.\nI = 200 × 314 × 40 × 10⁻⁶ = 2.5 A."}
];
